 # Deformation Field Parametrization
 
-Also called: Dynamic NeRF deformation, Canonical space mapping, Time-varying radiance fields
 
 ## Overview
-Deformation Field Parametrization addresses the limitation of static Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) by enabling the representation of dynamic, non-rigid scenes. It solves the problem of reconstructing time-varying geometry and appearance by mapping 3D spatial coordinates and time into a static "canonical" space, where a standard static NeRF can be applied [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from static NeRF variants is the explicit modeling of temporal evolution through a deformation function, allowing for the reconstruction of moving objects from sparse or multi-view data that would otherwise be incoherent in a static model.
+Deformation Field Parametrization addresses the problem of rendering dynamic, non-rigid scenes by mapping time-varying coordinates to a static canonical space, thereby decoupling geometry deformation from appearance [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Dynamic Scene Representation](13340-dynamic-scene-representation.md) by explicitly parameterizing the displacement field (e.g., via MLPs, splines, or hybrid models) rather than relying solely on explicit mesh tracking or full spatio-temporal neural networks, allowing for topology-preserving motion modeling with lower memory overhead [S1, unofficial].
 
