-### Neural Deformation Field
-A neural network $F_{\Delta}$ that predicts per-point displacement $\Delta$ in canonical space to accommodate non-rigid deformation, conditioned on target pose $\theta_t$ and signed height $\tilde{h}$ [S3, unofficial].
-
-The canonical point $\mathbf{x}_c$ is obtained by combining inverse Linear Blend Skinning (LBS) deformation and neural displacement:
-$$
-\mathbf{x}_c = LBS^{-1}(\mathbf{x}_t) + \Delta
-$$
-where $\Delta = F_{\Delta}(\phi(\tilde{h}), \theta_t)$ [S3, unofficial]. Here, $\phi(\cdot)$ represents a position encoding function [44-radiance-field-optimization.md], and $\tilde{h}$ is a signed height indicator derived from the signed distance $\bar{d}$ of the point to the mesh surface [S3, unofficial].
-
-This method is chosen when joint optimization of geometry and texture is required without manual parameter tuning, allowing for editing of pose, geometry, and texture [S3, unofficial]. It specifically handles non-rigid deformation that LBS alone cannot capture [S3, unofficial].
+#### step
+- [S2, unofficial] Estimate location rotation for each point during deformation using Singular Value Decomposition (SVD), borrowing the idea from ARAP.
+- [S2, unofficial] Interpolate the estimated per-point rotation to the query view direction of the pre-trained radiance field.
+- [S2, unofficial] Use the interpolated rotation to redirect the querying ray in the right direction, ensuring consistency between deformed and canonical space representations.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of using canonical models and deformation fields originated in the context of dynamic tomography and neural rendering. Early approaches parametrized temporal evolution using discrete timesteps with linear interpolation or continuous grid displacements obtained from MLPs [S1, unofficial]. The drawback of canonical models is their inability to handle topological changes, which necessitates spatio-temporal networks or regularizers like elastic regularization, though these can be problematic for topological variations [S1, unofficial].
-
-In the broader context of 3D vision, this paradigm was established for animating articulated bodies using a baseline skeleton with MLP-based deformation fields, inspiring works such as A-NeRF and Animatable NeRF [S2]. RigNeRF specifically innovated on this by using deformation fields guided by morphable 3D face models [S2]. More recent developments like UVA utilize neural deformation fields conditioned on pose and signed height to jointly optimize geometry fields from scratch [S3, unofficial].
+The concept of using canonical models with deformation fields was introduced to address the limitations of full spatio-temporal neural radiance fields, which can be ill-conditioned and require complex regularizers [S1, unofficial]. Early approaches parametrized temporal evolution using discrete timesteps with linear interpolation or continuous grid displacements obtained from MLPs [S1, unofficial]. The drawback of canonical models is their inability to handle topological changes, necessitating spatio-temporal networks or low-rank decomposition for such cases [S1, unofficial]. Recent developments include spline-based parametrizations for sparse data reconstruction [S1, unofficial] and local rotation estimation via SVD to handle large rotations in point-based radiance fields [S2, unofficial].
 
